  "worked_solution": "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- $f(-2)=6,$ so $f(-2)$ exists. $\u2713$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}({x}^{3}-x) \\\\  & =(-2)^{3}-(-2) \\\\  & =-8+2 \\\\  & =-6, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(-{x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =-(-2)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =-4-2 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=-6$ $\u2713$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=-6,$ while $f(-2)=6.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
